(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-vignettes"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with newly diagnosed obstructive sleep apnea also has heart failure and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. She asks for the adjustable device a friend uses. Which device should she not be given?","choices":["Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Continuous positive airway pressure","Nasal expiratory resistance device","Oral appliance worn during sleep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Autotitrating (adjustable) positive airway pressure is used in patients without significant comorbid conditions. Her heart failure and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease are significant comorbidities, so she should get continuous positive airway pressure, the most effective option, instead. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has a body mass index of 36 kg/m2. He asks what he can do himself, in addition to using his positive airway pressure device. Which risk modification fits him?","choices":["Sleeping on his back each night","A nightly sedative to deepen sleep","An evening glass of wine to relax","Diet and exercise for weight loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity heads the risk factors for obstructive sleep apnea, and diet and exercise for weight loss is a named risk modification, together with avoiding alcohol and other sedatives. Bariatric surgery for weight loss may also be helpful. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 12, 13, 15"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness. During 6 hours of sleep his polysomnogram records 48 apneas, 42 hypopneas and 36 respiratory effort-related arousals, predominantly obstructive. What is his apnea-hypopnea index?","choices":["7 events per hour","8 events per hour","15 events per hour","21 events per hour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The apnea-hypopnea index is (apneas + hypopneas) divided by total sleep time in hours: (48 + 42) / 6 = 90 / 6 = 15 events per hour. Respiratory effort-related arousals belong to the respiratory disturbance index, not to this one. At 15 or more predominantly obstructive events, the diagnosis is confirmed. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness, and his primary care clinician suspects obstructive sleep apnea. Which referral is part of his care?","choices":["Maxillofacial surgeon","Cardiologist","Sleep physician","Bariatric surgeon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for obstructive sleep apnea includes referral to a sleep physician, together with proper counseling. His snoring, witnessed apneas and sleepiness make the diagnosis likely, and confirming it takes a sleep study. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 15, 13, 18"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman with habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness has a home sleep apnea test that does not show obstructive sleep apnea. Her symptoms persist. What is the best next step?","choices":["Reassurance","Repeat home test","Modafinil","Polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits will not always diagnose mild obstructive sleep apnea; they are most helpful in moderate to severe disease. A negative home study in a woman with ongoing snoring and sleepiness therefore needs a polysomnogram, which can also find other sleep disorders a home kit cannot assess. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 11"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with heart failure has insomnia and frequent awakenings, and central sleep apnea is suspected. Which study establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Polysomnography","Home sleep apnea test","Arterial blood gas","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is diagnosed by polysomnography, and at-home testing is not sufficient. The study shows repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion, and heart failure is also a reason to use a polysomnogram rather than a home kit. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 8, 32"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has severe obstructive sleep apnea on polysomnogram. She finds her mask uncomfortable and asks whether an oral appliance would work as well. What should she be told?","choices":["It works if she sleeps on her back","It equals the mask if worn nightly","It is not effective in severe disease","It is the most effective option"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An oral appliance is not effective in severe obstructive sleep apnea or in patients without teeth. Her disease is severe, so the appliance is not a substitute; continuous positive airway pressure remains the most effective treatment for her. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman with a body mass index of 44 kg/m2 has daytime hypersomnolence, loud snoring and dyspnea on exertion. Awake on room air, her arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 54 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Her chest radiograph, thyroid function tests, pulmonary function tests and toxicology screen show no other cause of hypoventilation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Obstructive sleep apnea alone","Central sleep apnea","Narcolepsy","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion made when obesity (body mass index above 30) and awake alveolar hypoventilation (arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg) are present and alternative causes of hypercapnia are excluded. Her 44 kg/m2, 54 mm Hg and negative workup meet all three. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 24, 10"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea declines treatment and asks how the condition affects his heart. Which risk is raised 3 to 6 times in untreated disease?","choices":["Hypertension","Driving accidents","Sudden cardiac death at night","Sudden cardiac death during exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries a 3- to 6-fold risk of sudden cardiac death at night, alongside high mortality from heart failure. The timing is the point: the excess risk falls during sleep, when the apneas occur. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 15"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with newly diagnosed obstructive sleep apnea drinks several glasses of wine each night and takes an over-the-counter sedative to fall asleep. Which advice addresses these modifiable risk factors?","choices":["Avoid alcohol and other sedatives","Keep the sedative, stop the wine","Keep the wine, stop the sedative","Take both earlier in the evening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Use of alcohol or sedatives is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, and the risk modifications are to avoid alcohol and other sedatives, along with diet and exercise for weight loss. Both his nightly wine and his sleep aid fall under that advice. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 15"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 41 kg/m2 has loud snoring and daytime sleepiness. His awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 49 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Which sleep study should be avoided in his evaluation?","choices":["Home sleep apnea kit","Polysomnography with carbon dioxide monitoring","In-laboratory polysomnogram","Pressure titration once diagnosed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In patients with awake hypoventilation a polysomnogram is used, not an at-home kit, and home kits assess obstructive sleep apnea without other sleep disorders. His awake carbon dioxide of 49 mm Hg is awake hypoventilation, and the gold standard for suspected obesity hypoventilation is polysomnography with continuous carbon dioxide monitoring. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 32"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with moderate obstructive sleep apnea cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure despite several mask changes. Which option is described for his severity and his intolerance of the device?","choices":["Palatal implants","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Modafinil","Radiofrequency ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Radiofrequency ablation is used in patients with mild to moderate obstructive sleep apnea who cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure. His disease is moderate and he cannot tolerate the device, which matches both conditions. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 26"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with long-standing untreated obstructive sleep apnea declines therapy. Which cardiovascular effect is expected from his untreated disease?","choices":["Higher risk of aortic dissection","Reduced blood pressure variability","Reduced sympathetic tone","Fewer cardiac arrhythmias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cardiovascular effects of untreated obstructive sleep apnea include increased sympathetic tone, bradycardia, increased blood pressure variability, increased arrhythmias and an increased risk of aortic aneurysms and aortic dissections. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 21, 18"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man's polysomnogram shows repeated events in which thermistor airflow falls by 90% or more for 15 seconds while chest and abdominal respiratory effort continue. How is each event classified?","choices":["Hypopnea","Obstructive apnea","Central apnea","Mixed apnea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An apnea is a 90% or greater fall in airflow on the thermistor lasting at least 10 seconds, and when airflow stops but respiratory effort continues, the apnea is obstructive. The airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe, the event pattern of obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with newly diagnosed obesity hypoventilation syndrome asks to start a weight-loss medication instead of a diet and exercise program. What should she be told?","choices":["Medication follows failed diet and exercise","Medication is the first-line therapy","Medication can replace pressure therapy","Surgery must come before medication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medications for obesity hypoventilation syndrome are reserved for those who fail diet and exercise measures. First line is a weight loss program with lifestyle changes (diet, exercise, behavioral modification) plus positive airway pressure, within a multidisciplinary approach. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has daytime sleepiness, and her partner reports habitual snoring and pauses in her breathing. Her polysomnogram shows 8 predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour, and her symptoms are not explained by another condition, a medication or a substance. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Diagnostic of obstructive sleep apnea","Not diagnostic until 15 events per hour","Diagnostic of central sleep apnea","Indeterminate until a home kit is done"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is confirmed with 5 or more predominantly obstructive events per hour plus a symptom such as sleepiness, or habitual snoring and breathing interruptions noted by a bed partner, when no other condition, medication or substance explains them. She has 8 events and those symptoms. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 8, 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with loud snoring and fatigue has an 8-hour polysomnogram that records 40 apneas, 32 hypopneas and 40 respiratory effort-related arousals. What is her respiratory disturbance index?","choices":["4 events per hour","5 events per hour","9 events per hour","14 events per hour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The respiratory disturbance index is (apneas + hypopneas + respiratory effort-related arousals) divided by total sleep time in hours: (40 + 32 + 40) / 8 = 112 / 8 = 14 events per hour. The arousals are obstructive airflow reductions that are neither apneas nor hypopneas. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man started modafinil for residual daytime sleepiness from obstructive sleep apnea. Two weeks later he develops a widespread rash and feels unwell. Which adverse effect of the drug best fits?","choices":["Worsening of his sleep apnea","Stimulant-induced headache","Drug hypersensitivity reaction","Stimulant nervousness and anxiety"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Listed side effects of modafinil and armodafinil include headaches, nervousness or anxiety, dizziness, nausea, and dermatologic and systemic hypersensitivities. A widespread rash with feeling unwell after starting the drug fits a dermatologic and systemic hypersensitivity reaction. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a body mass index of 41 kg/m2 and obstructive sleep apnea is being evaluated, and her clinician's suspicion of obesity hypoventilation syndrome is low to moderate. Her serum bicarbonate is 31 mEq/L (normal 22-29). What is the next step?","choices":["Repeat serum bicarbonate","Home sleep apnea test","Diagnose the syndrome now","Arterial blood gas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When suspicion is low to moderate, a serum bicarbonate above 27 means a higher chance of obesity hypoventilation syndrome and the need for an arterial blood gas. Her 31 mEq/L crosses that line, and the gas measures the awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure the diagnosis requires (above 45 mm Hg). — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 31, 8"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman snores loudly and is treated for high blood pressure. She denies daytime tiredness, and no one has seen her stop breathing. Her body mass index is 32 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 16 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 2, low risk","Score 3, intermediate risk","Score 3, high risk","Score 4, high risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed 0 + treated pressure 1 + body mass index 32 (not above 35) 0 + age 42 0 + neck 16 inches 1 + female 0 = 3. By count that is intermediate, but yes to 2 or more of the first 4 questions plus a neck of 16 inches / 40 cm is a high-risk rule, so she is high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has central sleep apnea after a stroke. His heart function is normal, he takes no opioids and he lives at sea level. A trial of continuous positive airway pressure has failed. What is the next therapy to consider?","choices":["Adaptive servo-ventilation","Reducing opioid medications","Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Bilevel positive airway pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In central sleep apnea, continuous positive airway pressure is considered first, and if it fails, adaptive servo-ventilation is next: it monitors breathing patterns in real time and delivers and adjusts pressure as needed. With normal heart function he lacks the reduced ejection fraction that makes it unsafe. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 12, 33"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman snores loudly. She feels rested during the day, no one has seen her stop breathing, and she is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 36 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 14 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 1, low risk","Score 2, low risk","Score 2, high risk","Score 3, intermediate risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed 0 + pressure 0 + body mass index 36 (more than 35) 1 + age 35 0 + neck 14 inches 0 + female 0 = 2, the low-risk band (0 to 2). The body mass index rule needs 2 or more yes answers to the first 4 questions, and she has 1, so she stays low risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman's polysomnogram shows events with no airflow. In each, there is no respiratory effort during the first part of the event, and effort resumes during the latter part while airflow is still absent. How are these events classified?","choices":["Obstructive apnea","Central apnea","Hypopnea","Mixed apnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A mixed apnea has no airflow, with a lack of respiratory effort during the first part of the event and resumption of effort during the latter part. Her events begin like central apneas and end like obstructive ones, which is exactly the mixed pattern. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a body mass index of 42 kg/m2 has obesity hypoventilation syndrome, confirmed by exclusion. Her husband has witnessed few apneas, and polysomnography with continuous carbon dioxide monitoring shows sleep-related hypoventilation. Which first-line treatment fits her?","choices":["Bariatric surgery as first therapy","Weight loss plus bilevel positive airway pressure","Weight loss plus continuous positive airway pressure","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a weight loss program with positive airway pressure, and the device follows the overlap: with sleep-related hypoventilation it is bilevel positive airway pressure. Her few witnessed apneas, older age and female sex fit that 10% subgroup. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman developed central sleep apnea after a stroke. She takes no opioids, has normal heart function and lives at sea level. Which therapy is considered first?","choices":["Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Continuous positive airway pressure","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Bilevel positive airway pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of central sleep apnea addresses an underlying cause (heart failure, opioids or altitude), and none applies to her. The next step is to consider continuous positive airway pressure first, with adaptive servo-ventilation held for when it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 24, 12, 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness. His insurer suggests a home sleep apnea kit. Which test should be used instead?","choices":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone level","In-laboratory polysomnogram","Home sleep apnea kit at a later date","Positive airway pressure titration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits suit uncomplicated suspected obstructive sleep apnea, but in significant cardiopulmonary disease a polysomnogram is used, not an at-home kit. His heart failure and chronic lung disease are that comorbidity, and heart failure is also a risk factor for central apnea, which a home kit cannot assess. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 24"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has not been helped by continuous positive airway pressure or an oral appliance. He asks which operation would be a cure. Which procedure is described as a cure for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Palatal implants","Radiofrequency ablation","Tracheostomy","Bariatric surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tracheostomy is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea because the site of upper airway obstruction is bypassed. Breathing no longer depends on the narrowed upper airway that collapses during sleep. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and central sleep apnea. His heart failure therapy has been optimized, and continuous positive airway pressure has failed. Which therapy should be avoided because of his heart failure?","choices":["Repeat polysomnography","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Continuous positive airway pressure","Optimized heart failure therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adaptive servo-ventilation is the usual step after continuous positive airway pressure fails, but it is not used for every patient, especially in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: with poor left ventricular function, reducing preload can decrease cardiac output. Cheyne-Stokes breathing can also be protective. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who has taken opioid medications for chronic back pain for years has snoring, frequent awakenings and daytime sleepiness. Which sleep study fits her?","choices":["Positive airway pressure titration","Polysomnogram in a sleep laboratory","Home sleep apnea kit","Questionnaire score before any study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Long-term opioid use is one of the settings where a polysomnogram, not an at-home kit, is used. Opioid medications are also a risk factor for central sleep apnea, which home kits cannot assess, since they assess obstructive sleep apnea and not other sleep disorders. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 16, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 37 kg/m2 has an awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 51 mm Hg (normal 35-45). He has not yet had a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests or a toxicology screen. What must happen before obesity hypoventilation syndrome can be diagnosed?","choices":["Document coexisting obstructive apnea","Confirm a body mass index above 35","Start bilevel pressure therapy first","Exclude other causes of hypercapnia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion: obesity (body mass index above 30) and awake hypercapnia (arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg) must be joined by exclusion of alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation. The workup includes a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests and a toxicology screen. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 31, 33, 17"}]};
